"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][15]={"school_physics_en:M15-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M15-L1 – Mechanical work, energy and conservative forces<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Conservation laws, collisions and gravitation<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A spring is extended from 10 cm to 20 cm. Does doubling the extension\ndouble the required work? The force law gives a different answer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Mechanical work<\/strong> represents energy transfer by a\nforce acting along a displacement. For a constant force,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=Fs\\cos\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a variable force, work is the signed area under the\nforce–displacement graph. Its unit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWork is positive when the force acts along the displacement and negative\nwhen it opposes motion.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>conservative force<\/strong> does work between two positions\nthat is independent of the path taken. It has an associated potential\nenergy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{conservative}}=-\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNear Earth’s surface, for an approximately uniform gravitational field,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_g=mgh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to a selected zero level. An ideal spring of stiffness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand extension\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{spring}}=-kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand stores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s=\\tfrac12kx^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe negative sign means the spring force points toward equilibrium.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Pay attention to whose work is being calculated.<\/strong> The\nspring’s work is the opposite of the work done by the person slowly\nstretching it. With a quasistatic motion and negligible losses,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{external}}=+\\Delta U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{spring}}=-\\Delta U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFriction cannot be represented by a position-only potential energy in\nthis simple model: its work depends on the path length.<\/p>\n<p>Changing the zero height shifts numerical potential energies but not\ntheir differences. If force is not constant, integrate it or estimate\nthe area from measurements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_089_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Force-extension graph of a linear spring from 0 to 20 cm; the 10-to-20 cm area represents 3 J.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Force-extension graph of a linear spring\nfrom 0 to 20 cm; the 10-to-20 cm area represents 3 J.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Green trapezoid area is 3 J: external work stretching slowly,\nwhile the spring performs −3 J.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal spring with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=200\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis stretched from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=0.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFirst,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.10<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1=\\tfrac12\\cdot200\\cdot0.10^2=1\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nnext,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_2=4\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe stored energy increases by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe hands slowly stretching it do\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+3\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof work, while the spring does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBetween 0.10 and 0.20 m, the force-magnitude graph contains a trapezoid\nof heights 20 N and 40 N:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(20+40)\\cdot0.10/2=3\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Testable sketch:<\/strong> Plot force magnitudes at 0, 5, 10,\n15 and 20 cm for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=200\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCompare the areas over 0–10 cm and 10–20 cm without physically\nstretching a strong spring. Use the original figure to discuss the\nquantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find spring energy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=200\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.15\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How much external work is needed to stretch the spring slowly from 0\nto 0.10 m?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to spring energy when extension is doubled at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmass rises by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith model value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.15<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s=\\tfrac12\\cdot200\\cdot0.15^2=2.25\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{external}}=1\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It becomes four times larger because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U_g=mgh=2\\cdot10\\cdot3=60\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Hooke’s law applies to a spring within its ideal linear-elastic\nrange. Material hysteresis, internal friction, and permanent deformation\nare omitted. The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mgh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodel assumes approximately constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ngravitational potential differs at large altitudes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L7<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://lehrplannavigator.nrw.de/system/files/media/document/file/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26, 28:\nintroductory mechanics and circular motion/gravitation; conservation and\ncollisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 21–22:\noptional introductory-phase momentum and energy; gravity also covered in\nqualification phase 3.2.1 · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-1-work\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 7.1 Work<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 7.1: work by constant and\nvariable forces · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-1-potential-energy-of-a-system\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 8.1 Potential Energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 8.1: spring\nand gravitational potential energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-2-conservative-and-non-conservative-forces\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 8.2 Conservative Forces<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 8.2:\nconservative forces and path independence · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M15-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M15-L2 – Conservation of energy across several stages<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Conservation laws, collisions and gravitation<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A cart descends a ramp and brakes along a rough section. Its final\nkinetic energy is less than the initial gravitational potential energy.\nWhere did the difference go?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In an <strong>isolated system<\/strong>, total energy is conserved. It\ncan be transferred between gravitational potential energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nkinetic energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=\\tfrac12mv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ninternal energy, and other forms. However, <strong>mechanical\nenergy<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{mech}=K+U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant only if nonconservative interactions do not convert or\ntransfer mechanical energy.<\/p>\n<p>Begin with a <strong>system boundary<\/strong>. For a cart, Earth’s\ngravitational interaction, and a rough track, we can write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{\\mathrm{start}}+K_{\\mathrm{start}}=U_{\\mathrm{finish}}+K_{\\mathrm{finish}}+\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{thermal}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif no other transfers occur. Equivalently,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta(K+U)=W_{\\mathrm{nonconservative}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNegative work by friction does not mean that energy was destroyed: the\ninternal energy of the contacting bodies increases.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>Sankey diagram<\/strong> displays energy flows in joules,\nwith arrow widths proportional to the energy amounts under a single\nscale. Report a percentage as an efficiency only if <em>useful\noutput<\/em> and a relevant input energy are clearly defined. Energy is\nnot simply ‘used up’: it is transferred or dispersed into other\nforms.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_090_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Flow with 60 J input, 48 J kinetic energy, and 12 J internal energy; proportional band widths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Flow with 60 J input, 48 J kinetic\nenergy, and 12 J internal energy; proportional band widths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>60 J of gravitational potential energy becomes 48 J kinetic and\n12 J internal energy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncart starts from rest at height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe explicitly use the simplified\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nignore rotation and take the final height as zero. Its initial\ngravitational potential energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong the track,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes internal energy. Thus the cart reaches the bottom with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=60-12=48\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=\\tfrac12mv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>48<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>48<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.93<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{2\\cdot48/2}=\\sqrt{48}\\approx6.93\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mechanical subsystem loses 12 J; the total system does not.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Energy-flow check:<\/strong> Draw a 60 J input arrow and 48 J\nand 12 J outgoing arrows with appropriate widths. State whether your\nsystem includes the cart alone or the cart, Earth, and track. Identify a\npossible measurement for each energy amount.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the starting potential energy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=4\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Out of 80 J, 20 J is transferred to internal energy. What kinetic\nenergy remains if nothing else changes?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What speed corresponds to 60 J of kinetic energy for a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbody?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the statement “friction destroys energy” correct?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">80\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=60\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>40<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.32<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{2K/m}=\\sqrt{40}\\approx6.32\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Mechanical energy is transferred to other forms, especially\ninternal energy; total energy is conserved.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Rolling wheels also store rotational energy. Sound, deformation, and\nthermal transfer to the environment can add further flows. The example\nis an intentionally simplified numerical model, not an actual\nmeasurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://lehrplannavigator.nrw.de/system/files/media/document/file/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26, 28:\nintroductory mechanics and circular motion/gravitation; conservation and\ncollisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 21–22:\noptional introductory-phase momentum and energy; gravity also covered in\nqualification phase 3.2.1 · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-3-conservation-of-energy\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 8.3 Conservation of Energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 8.3:\nenergy balances and nonconservative work · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-1-potential-energy-of-a-system\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 8.1 Potential Energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 8.1: spring\nand gravitational potential energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M15-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M15-L3 – Momentum and its conservation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Conservation laws, collisions and gravitation<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two dynamics carts join after a collision. Why is their common speed\nlower even though total momentum remains unchanged?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A body’s <strong>momentum<\/strong> is the vector quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec p=m\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}=\\mathrm{N\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn one dimension signs encode direction; in multiple dimensions analyse\nseparate components. <strong>Impulse<\/strong> is the change in momentum:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>J<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec J=\\Delta\\vec p=\\int\\vec F\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a constant force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>J<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec J=\\vec F\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For several bodies,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>J<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\vec p_\\mathrm{total}=\\vec J_\\mathrm{external}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen the external impulse over the time interval is negligible, total\nmomentum is conserved:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i m_i\\vec v_{i,\\mathrm{before}}=\\sum_i m_i\\vec v_{i,\\mathrm{after}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInternal collision forces occur in equal-and-opposite pairs and cancel\nfrom the total balance, provided both bodies are included within the\nsystem boundary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Conservation of momentum does not imply conservation of\nkinetic energy.<\/strong> If objects deform or remain attached after\ncollision, part of the initial kinetic energy can become internal energy\nor sound. Total momentum can remain constant when external impulse is\nsmall. Work out separate momentum and energy balances rather than\nconfusing them.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_091_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two carts, 1 kg at 4 m/s and 3 kg at rest, then both 1 m/s; total momentum 4 kg m/s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two carts, 1 kg at 4 m/s and 3 kg at\nrest, then both 1 m/s; total momentum 4 kg m/s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>With negligible external impulse, total momentum remains 4 kg m/s\nwhile kinetic energy decreases.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_1=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncart moving at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=+4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhits a stationary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_2=3\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncart. They couple and move together. Initially,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\mathrm{before}=1\\cdot4+3\\cdot0=+4\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfterwards,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+3)v=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=+1\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nKinetic energy was initially\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is finally\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\cdot4\\cdot1^2=2\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconverted to other forms. The individual cart momenta are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+4\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore the collision, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+3\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafterwards.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Model comparison:<\/strong> Simulate different cart masses in\na single horizontal direction, make external impulse negligible, and\ncheck the sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\mathrm{before}-p_\\mathrm{after}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA real demonstration must use slow, supervised school equipment\nonly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the momentum of a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncart moving at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=-3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nforce acts rightward for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncarts moving at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncouple. Find their final velocity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is kinetic energy not necessarily conserved when carts stick\ntogether?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2\\cdot(-3)=-6\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p=F\\Delta t=+2\\,\\mathrm{N\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(2\\cdot3+2\\cdot(-1))/4=+1\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Deformation, thermal energy and sound preserve total energy, but may\nreduce kinetic energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A long impact or strong friction with the floor may invalidate the\nnegligible-external-impulse assumption. For a system containing only one\ncart, the other cart exerts an external force; for the combined two-cart\nsystem it is internal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://lehrplannavigator.nrw.de/system/files/media/document/file/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26, 28:\nintroductory mechanics and circular motion/gravitation; conservation and\ncollisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 21–22:\noptional introductory-phase momentum and energy; gravity also covered in\nqualification phase 3.2.1 · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-3-conservation-of-linear-momentum\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 9.3 Conservation of Momentum<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 9.3:\ntotal momentum and external forces · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-2-impulse-and-collisions\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 9.2 Impulse and Collisions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 9.2:\nimpulse and change in momentum · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M15-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M15-L4 – Elastic and inelastic collisions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Conservation laws, collisions and gravitation<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two identical carts collide. In one case, the incoming cart passes\nits motion to the other; in a second case, the carts join. What is\nconserved in each?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A collision is <strong>elastic<\/strong> when both the total momentum\nand the total kinetic energy of the isolated two-body system are\nconserved. An <strong>inelastic<\/strong> collision conserves momentum\n(without external impulse) but not kinetic energy. In a\n<strong>perfectly inelastic<\/strong> collision the two bodies share the\nfinal velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(m_1v_1+m_2v_2)/(m_1+m_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>In a central, one-dimensional, ideally elastic collision of two equal\nmasses, with one initially at rest, the bodies exchange velocities. This\nfollows from solving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\mathrm{before}=p_\\mathrm{after}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_\\mathrm{before}=K_\\mathrm{after}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntogether. For equal masses in a perfectly inelastic collision, the\nshared final velocity is half the incoming cart’s speed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Total energy<\/strong> is still conserved in an inelastic\ncollision. It is the total <em>kinetic<\/em> energy that decreases:\ndeformation, internal energy, and sound account for the difference.\nVehicle crashes are not appropriate experiments for learners; slow\neducational carts and simulations can demonstrate these ideas\nsafely.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_092_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two equal carts, initially 2 m/s and 0; elastic: 0 and 2 m/s; coupled: both at 1 m/s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two equal carts, initially 2 m/s and 0;\nelastic: 0 and 2 m/s; coupled: both at 1 m/s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Total momentum stays 2 kg m/s in both cases; kinetic energy\nremains constant only for the elastic collision.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A cart of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_1=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=+2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstrikes an identical stationary cart. <strong>Ideal elastic:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{1,\\mathrm{after}}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{2,\\mathrm{after}}=+2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand kinetic energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=2\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare unchanged. <strong>Perfectly inelastic:<\/strong> Both move at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{after}}=(1\\cdot2+1\\cdot0)/2=1\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMomentum remains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_\\mathrm{after}=\\tfrac12\\cdot2\\cdot1^2=1\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwent into other forms of energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>A table, not a crash:<\/strong> For each model, list both\nvelocities, total momentum, and total kinetic energy before and after.\nDraw momentum arrows with the same scale. Does the observation “the cart\nbounced” alone prove that the collision is elastic?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two equal-mass carts have velocities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore an ideal central elastic collision. What are the final\nvelocities?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What shared final velocity results for the same carts in a perfectly\ninelastic collision?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For two\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncarts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhow much kinetic energy is converted in a perfectly inelastic\ncollision?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is total energy of an isolated system conserved during an inelastic\ncollision?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{1,\\mathrm{after}}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{2,\\mathrm{after}}=+3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{after}}=+1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta K=2-1=1\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconverted rather than destroyed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes. Energy is transferred, for instance, to deformation, internal\nenergy, and sound.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The velocity-exchange rule requires equal masses and an ideal head-on\none-dimensional elastic collision. Real impacts take time and can be\noblique, rotating, and frictional.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://lehrplannavigator.nrw.de/system/files/media/document/file/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26, 28:\nintroductory mechanics and circular motion/gravitation; conservation and\ncollisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 21–22:\noptional introductory-phase momentum and energy; gravity also covered in\nqualification phase 3.2.1 · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-4-types-of-collisions\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 9.4 Types of Collisions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 9.4:\nelastic and inelastic collisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-3-conservation-of-linear-momentum\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 9.3 Conservation of Momentum<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 9.3:\ntotal momentum and external forces · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M15-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M15-L5 – Gravity and orbital motion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Conservation laws, collisions and gravitation<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a satellite not simply crash into Earth, even though gravity\ncontinuously attracts it? Its tangential motion and the curvature of its\npath must be considered together.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Two point masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseparated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nattract with magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G=GmM/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.6743<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G\\approx6.6743\\cdot10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m^2/kg^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOutside a spherically symmetric mass distribution, the full mass may be\ntreated as concentrated at its centre. The distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured from the <strong>centre of Earth<\/strong>, not from its\nsurface.<\/p>\n<p>For an <strong>ideal circular orbit<\/strong>, gravity supplies the\nrequired centripetal force:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">GmM/r^2=mv^2/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCancelling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{GM/r}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand orbital period\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi r/v=2\\pi\\sqrt{r^3/(GM)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this simple model, orbital speed does not depend on the satellite’s\nown mass.<\/p>\n<p>The satellite is in <strong>free fall<\/strong>: velocity is tangent\nto the path, whereas acceleration points inward to the centre. ‘In\norbit’ does not mean ‘outside gravity’. The model neglects residual\natmospheric drag in low orbits, Earth’s flattening, and perturbations by\nother bodies. The drawn circular path is not to scale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_093_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Earth as a circle with satellite on schematic orbit; tangential velocity arrow and inward gravitational force arrow.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Earth as a circle with satellite on\nschematic orbit; tangential velocity arrow and inward gravitational\nforce arrow.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Satellite velocity is tangential while acceleration points toward\nEarth’s centre.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For an Earth-orbit model, take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.986<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">GM\\approx3.986\\cdot10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{m^3/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand orbital radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=7.00\\cdot10^6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom Earth’s centre. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7546<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{GM/r}\\approx7546\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe circumference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>7.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7546<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5829<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>97.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\approx2\\pi\\cdot7.00\\cdot10^6/7546\\approx5829\\,\\mathrm s\\approx97.1\\,\\mathrm{min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEarth has an approximate mean radius of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.37<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.37\\cdot10^6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmaking this model orbit roughly 630 km above the surface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Digital-model variation:<\/strong> Double\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile holding the central mass fixed. Compare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom their power laws. Label a tangent velocity arrow and an inward\nradial acceleration arrow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to gravitational force when the separation doubles at\nfixed masses?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to circular orbital speed when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By what factor does period\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when orbital radius doubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=R_\\mathrm{Earth}+h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe used for a satellite near Earth?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to one quarter:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G\\propto r^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls by a factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\propto r^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It increases by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.83<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{3/2}=2\\sqrt2\\approx2.83<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Gravity uses centre-to-centre separation, not height above the\nsurface.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Accurate orbital calculations may require a nonspherical gravity\nmodel, atmospheric drag, and third-body perturbations. A circular orbit\ncannot describe arbitrary launch conditions or eccentric paths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://lehrplannavigator.nrw.de/system/files/media/document/file/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26, 28:\nintroductory mechanics and circular motion/gravitation; conservation and\ncollisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 21–22:\noptional introductory-phase momentum and energy; gravity also covered in\nqualification phase 3.2.1 · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-1-newtons-law-of-universal-gravitation\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 13.1 Universal Gravitation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 13.1:\ngravitational force and dependence on separation · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-4-satellite-orbits-and-energy\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 13.4 Satellite Orbits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 13.4:\ncircular orbit, speed and period · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M15-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M15-L6 – Limits of classical models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Conservation laws, collisions and gravitation<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>The standard near-surface fall equation often works well in a\nclassroom. Why is the same calculation unsuitable for a satellite, even\nthough both objects are attracted by Earth?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>model<\/strong> is a justified simplification. State the\nsystem boundary, target observable, assumptions, and range of validity\nbefore applying it. Over short distances near Earth’s surface,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\approx\\mathrm{constant}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis often adequate. At greater heights, local gravitational acceleration\nfollows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(r)=GM/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2R_\\mathrm{Earth}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(r)=g(R_\\mathrm{Earth})/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>point-mass model<\/strong> ignores size, rotation, material\nproperties, and internal energy. It can be very effective for tracking\ncentre-of-mass motion; in collisions, deformation, sound, and angular\nmomentum may matter. An ideally elastic collision is a limiting case,\nnot a promise about real carts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A measurement discrepancy is not automatically a model\nfailure.<\/strong> Evaluate uncertainty and the measurement process\nbefore deciding whether a difference is statistically plausible or\nsystematic. Conversely, good agreement over a narrow range does not\nestablish universal validity. At much larger scales or extreme speeds,\nrelativity or quantum theory may become necessary; neither is an\nautomatic replacement for every classroom calculation.<\/p>\n<p>Compare models using the same measurable quantity, units, and\ncontrolled conditions. A good explanation includes a labelled sketch,\ngoverning equation, and explicit statement of assumptions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_094_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Near-surface gravitational acceleration g0 contrasted with g0/4 at distance 2R from Earth’s centre.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Near-surface gravitational acceleration\ng0 contrasted with g0/4 at distance 2R from Earth’s centre.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Doubling centre-to-centre distance reduces gravitational\nacceleration to one quarter.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Let Earth have radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand surface gravitational acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_0=GM/R^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe distance from Earth’s centre is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=R+h=2R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(h)=GM/(2R)^2=g_0/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith an illustrative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_0=9.8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.45<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(h)=2.45\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA\nconstant-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodel would be seriously wrong there; the radial dependence must be\nused. The figure compares accelerations, not fall times or actual\nsatellite measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Model workshop:<\/strong> Prepare a table for “falling through\n1 m”, “falling from great height”, and “colliding model carts”. Mark:\npoint masses? constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nnegligible friction? elastic collision? Identify one first measurement\nfor testing each assumption.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas a fraction of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=R/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas a fraction of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give two reasons why treating real colliding bodies as point masses\nmay be inadequate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does agreement at two measurement points prove that a model works\nover every possible range?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g/g_0=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1.5R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.444<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g/g_0=1/(1.5)^2=4/9\\approx0.444<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Possible reasons: deformation, rotation, friction, time-dependent\ncontact forces, and thermal transfer.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Agreement supports a model within its tested range only;\nuncertainty and alternatives remain relevant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequation assumes spherical symmetry and Newtonian gravity. Real Earth is\nnot perfectly spherical or homogeneous; rotation and orbital effects are\nnoted as limitations rather than calculated here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1 – Electric charge and the\nelectric field<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://lehrplannavigator.nrw.de/system/files/media/document/file/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26, 28:\nintroductory mechanics and circular motion/gravitation; conservation and\ncollisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 21–22:\noptional introductory-phase momentum and energy; gravity also covered in\nqualification phase 3.2.1 · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-1-newtons-law-of-universal-gravitation\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 13.1 Universal Gravitation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 13.1:\ngravitational force and dependence on separation · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-7-einsteins-theory-of-gravity\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 13.7 Einstein’s Theory of Gravity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n13.7: limits of Newtonian gravitation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/itl/sed/topic-areas/measurement-uncertainty\">NIST\n– Measurement Uncertainty<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Metrology reference: account for\nuncertainty before interpreting model residuals · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
